>
  <h2 id="what-it-does">What it does</h2>
  <p>
    karucode is a coding agent you run yourself — and the whole of it is one binary. Point it at a
    project and it reads the files, searches them, edits them, runs commands with streamed output,
    and inspects git. It delegates work to subagents, and it keeps going through a multi-step task
    until the work is done or you stop it.
  </p>
  <p>
    It is one Rust binary. State lives in a SQLite database in WAL mode — projects, sessions, runs
    and agents. The browser UI is embedded in that binary and served from the same origin, so there
    is no Node at runtime and nothing else to deploy.
  </p>
  <p>
    A run is not cut off by a turn counter. <code>max_iterations</code> defaults to
    <code>0</code>, which means no ceiling: the model works until the task is done, you cancel it,
    or the wall clock runs out.
  </p>

  <pre class="code kc-budget">{`max_iterations      0  no turn ceiling (default)
max_run_secs    86400  hard wall clock, 24 h
loop guard             stops a run repeating itself

a non-zero max_iterations is an optional backstop`}</pre>

  <p>
    Approvals sit in front of every mutation: <code>ask</code> is the default for shell commands and
    file writes. On restart, runs left in <code>running</code> become <code>interrupted</code> and
    pending approvals <code>expired</code>. Nothing is silently retried.
  </p>

  <h2 id="start-here">Start here</h2>
  <p>Three pages, in the order most people need them.</p>
  <StartHere items={START} />

  <h2 id="how-it-is-put-together">How it is put together</h2>
  <p>
    Five jobs and one database. Each one is a layer you can reason about on its own, which is the
    point of keeping the whole thing in a single binary.
  </p>

  <dl class="kc-jobs">
    <div class="kc-jobs__row">
      <dt>axum API + SSE</dt>
      <dd>
        Serves the REST API and the embedded UI from one origin. Runs and their events stream to the
        browser over SSE.
      </dd>
    </div>
    <div class="kc-jobs__row">
      <dt>agent loop</dt>
      <dd>
        Owns approvals, subagents and budgets. Independent tool calls run in parallel; anything that
        can see a sibling’s effects — shell, plugins, MCP, subagents, approvals — runs in call order,
        so the transcript is the one a sequential run would have produced.
      </dd>
    </div>
    <div class="kc-jobs__row">
      <dt>provider</dt>
      <dd>
        Any endpoint that speaks the OpenAI chat-completions API with streaming tool calls. Providers
        are install-wide; a session can pin its own model.
      </dd>
    </div>
    <div class="kc-jobs__row">
      <dt>tools</dt>
      <dd>Files, search, shell, git, and karucode’s own documentation — plus a PTY terminal tab
        for you, which the model cannot call.</dd>
    </div>
    <div class="kc-jobs__row">
      <dt>plugins + MCP</dt>
      <dd>
        Separate processes, namespaced as <code>plugin__&lt;name&gt;__&lt;tool&gt;</code> and
        <code>mcp__&lt;server&gt;__&lt;tool&gt;</code>, so a third-party tool cannot shadow a
        built-in one.
      </dd>
    </div>
    <div class="kc-jobs__row">
      <dt>SQLite (WAL)</dt>
      <dd>
        Projects, sessions, runs and agents. This is what makes a crash survivable: a restart resumes
        the same transcript, with unfinished runs marked <code>interrupted</code> rather than retried.
      </dd>
    </div>
  </dl>

  <p>
    The home page draws the same stack as a diagram, if you want the picture:
    <a href="/#architecture">architecture</a>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="what-it-refuses-to-do">What it refuses to do</h2>
  <p>
    These are properties of the design, not gaps waiting to be closed. They are listed here because
    a tool you expose to a network should tell you where its edges are.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>No shell sandbox.</strong> Commands run as the server user. Use a container or a VM for
      untrusted code, or for unattended <code>auto</code> mode.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>No built-in TLS.</strong> Put a reverse proxy in front for remote access; there is no
      certificate handling in the binary.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>No plugin sandbox.</strong> A plugin is a separate process with your permissions. A
      rebuilt plugin is picked up by a reload — <code>POST /api/plugins/reload</code>, or
      <code>plugins_watch</code> — rather than by restarting the server.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>No per-project providers</strong>, no team workspaces, no cloud sync, no hosted
      service, no signup.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    The full list lives on the home page under <a href="/#limitations">what it doesn’t do</a>. The
    trust boundaries — what is contained, what is not, and how to expose it — are on
    <a href="/security/">Security</a>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="requirements">Requirements</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Linux, x86_64</strong> for the published release. The installer needs
      <code>curl</code> and <code>sh</code>; the systemd path needs root.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>No compiler.</strong> The release is a prebuilt binary with the UI inside it — Rust
      1.89+ and Node 22.12+ are only needed to build from source.
    </li>
    <li>
      Optional: <code>git</code>, and <code>ripgrep</code> — a pure-Rust search fallback is built in.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    No cloud account, no signup, no telemetry. MIT licensed. Start with
    <a href="/docs/quickstart/">Quick start</a>.
  </p>
</Docs>

